00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.450
then all sequences
are equally likely.

00:07:56.450 --> 00:08:00.950
Each has a probability
of 1 over 2 to the n.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:07.250
But now these sequences come
with a bias, a probability

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:11.690
of a 1 being P. So what's
the probability of seeing

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:16.940
a particular sequence 0
0 1 1 0 0 1 1 if the way

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:22.070
that I generated these
sequences was IID Bernoulli p?

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:23.720
It depends on the number.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:24.290
Right.

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:25.420
How?

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:29.480
To the power of k multiplied
by 1 minus p [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:30.380
Exactly.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:34.200
So it's going to be p.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:35.690
So this is small p.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:39.530
So it's going to be in
fact exactly small p,

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:43.640
the small p that governs
the bias in these coin

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:52.400
flips, to the power let's just
write in words, number of ones

00:08:52.400 --> 00:09:07.170
in the sequence xn times 1 minus
p to the power number of zeros

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:07.770
in xn.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:15.510
Every one here gives me a
factor of p, every 0 gives me

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:19.290
a factor of 1 minus p.

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:25.200
And so approximately,
by these two properties,

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:28.350
this is going to be then what?

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:35.700
p this small p to the
power, a number of 1s

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:45.990
with high probability is
going to be about np times

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:52.560
1 minus p to the power of zeros,
which with high probability,

00:09:52.560 --> 00:10:03.050
is going to be n
times 1 minus P.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:06.230
So that's equal to what?

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:10.970
It's equal to, well, I can
always write this as two

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:17.030
to the power log base
two of the same thing.

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:21.110
Right so it's 2 to
the power log base 2

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:22.550
when I don't write the base.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:28.130
It's just base 2 to the power
log base 2 of the same thing.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:35.570
That's pn times p times
1 minus p and times 1

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:44.140
minus p, which is what?

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:56.190
2 to the power n, log
of or I have here the p

00:10:56.190 --> 00:11:01.920
comes here, p times log of--

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:04.440
in fact, let me put a--

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:07.940
let me do this.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.740
2 to the minus.

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:11.990
Let me pull out a minus here.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:20.560
So I have p times the minus
log of p, or in other words

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:33.740
log of 1 over p, plus 1 minus
p log of over 1 minus p.

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:47.030
Does this look familiar?

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:57.990
What is this in terms of a
quantity that you recently

00:11:57.990 --> 00:11:59.730
encountered and defined?

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:09.200
This is exactly the entropy
of these components,

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:14.120
of these random variable
x that describes them.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:19.500
It's p the entropy you
should remind ourselves,

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:24.170
in general, the entropy
of a random variable x

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:30.410
was defined as the sum over all
the possible values, small x,

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:34.430
this random variable can
take, its probability,

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:39.500
the probability of this small
x log 1 over the probability.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:43.850
In the case of
Bernoulli p, something

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:49.220
that has two possible values one
with probability P, so that's

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:54.180
the p log 1 over p, and
then the other value

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:57.930
the other possible value is 0
that has probability 1 minus p.

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:02.940
So that's the other value, 1
minus p log 1 over 1 minus p.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:10.850
So this is nothing but the
entropy of the source which

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:12.965
is generating your data.

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:23.110
So with high probability,
with all likelihood,

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:30.490
the sequence that you're going
to get is going to have--

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:31.900
when you look at
it, you're going

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:36.610
to ask, oh, what's the
probability, in retrospect,

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:38.890
that's the sequence that I got?

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:41.680
Well, it's
probability is about 2

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:44.500
to the minus n
times the entropy.

