these are not fair coin flips
but this is some p which is not

00:23:21.170 --> 00:23:25.280
a half, then what
can you tell me

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:30.820
about the entropy
relative to one?

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:32.455
Is it bigger or smaller?

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:36.590
Smaller.

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:40.340
Among all Bernoulli variables,
the one that has largest entropy

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:45.590
is Bernoulli half, and that's
going to have entropy one.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:51.620
So anything else which is biased
will have entropy less than 1,

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:55.860
and so this is not
fair coin flips.

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:57.650
Entropy is less than 1.

00:23:57.650 --> 00:23:59.960
The fraction of the
typical sequences

00:23:59.960 --> 00:24:05.450
that you might actually see with
any non-negligible probability

00:24:05.450 --> 00:24:11.460
is an exponentially
small fraction

00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:13.830
of all the possible sequences.

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:15.480
So this is a very big deal.

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:16.920
So this is not drawn to scale.

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:21.930
This thing is actually
minuscule, exponentially small

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:27.210
for large n or as n
becomes bigger and bigger,

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:32.460
and this has a really
fundamental ramification

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:34.950
for compression
because what it means

00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:41.520
is that I can and should
focus all my coding

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:46.530
efforts on this typical set.

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:50.250
I should think about how do I
actually design a compressor

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:53.280
under this assumption,
this value assumption

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:56.460
that with all
likelihood, the sequence

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:59.400
I'm going to have to represent
to describe to the decoder

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:04.320
is going to be here
in this small set.

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:14.640
So, in fact, who can suggest
based on this observation, who

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:20.940
can suggest a
coding scheme that's

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:23.340
going to get a
compressor, that's

00:25:23.340 --> 00:25:27.795
going to get us
close to the entropy.

00:25:49.170 --> 00:25:49.670
Anyone?

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:58.740
If I know that my source--

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:04.140
the sequence that I
will have to describe,

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:12.300
let's say I knew the probability
1 that the sequence that I'm

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:19.630
going to have to describe
is in this set, two sizes 2

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:22.690
to the nh, how many bits?

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:33.540
How many bits of description
am I going to need

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:35.570
If I'm going to
have to-- if I need

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:42.680
to describe one possible
sequence or one possible element

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:49.580
among a set of a certain size,
then as we've seen essentially

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:54.470
up to rounding off
the number of bits

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:57.080
that I need to basically
describe or index

00:26:57.080 --> 00:27:03.245
the possible elements in a set
of certain size is log the size.

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:19.570
So if I knew that my
sequence for sure is in here,

00:27:19.570 --> 00:27:22.960
I would need
essentially log the size

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:27.970
of the set, which is essentially
n times h bits describe it.

00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:31.870
But now I know that
with all likelihood,

00:27:31.870 --> 00:27:36.580
it's going to be here, and with
some negligible probability,

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:40.180
it's going to be something else.

00:27:40.180 --> 00:27:44.080
Then how can I use
that to construct

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:52.300
a maybe variable length code,
variable length compressor that

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:59.500
will come close to compressing
down to essentially

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:02.570
the entropy of the source?

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:03.790
Anyone want to try?

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:16.740
It's inside the set, then
the compressed string starts

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:19.470
with one and then
there's some sequence

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:22.830
of bits that just enumerates
the [INAUDIBLE] set,

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:25.620
and we know that's
n times h bits.

00:28:25.620 --> 00:28:28.080
And then, otherwise,
it starts with 0,

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:30.094
and then it's just [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:36.570
That sounds like a
pretty good idea.

00:28:36.570 --> 00:28:40.430
So let me try and recap.

00:28:40.430 --> 00:28:46.790
So what the encoder is
going to output is first

00:28:46.790 --> 00:28:59.150
basically one bit to indicate
whether this the source

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:02.750
sequence is typical--

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:13.310
to indicate whether the
sequence is typical or not.

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:16.640
So I'm going to see my
realized source sequence.

00:29:16.640 --> 00:29:19.430
I'm going to ask, wait,
is it in this set or not?

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:22.190
I'm going to first give you
output one bit to tell you

00:29:22.190 --> 00:29:23.510
whether or not it's typical.

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:30.590
If it's typical, then
after this first bit,

00:29:30.590 --> 00:29:37.380
I'm going to use
essentially an h

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:39.810
bit because I'm going
to use essentially

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:42.870
log the size of the
typical set bits.

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:58.110
That's all I need in order to
describe one element in this set

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:00.720
to describe it.

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:02.430
That's if it's typical.

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:06.600
So if it was typical,
then the decoder

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:10.260
knows now that we're focusing
on-- you're describing me

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:14.340
now just one
possibility from here,

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:20.590
and I need just log the size
of this set to describe it.

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:23.820
But if that bit indicated
that it's not here

00:30:23.820 --> 00:30:32.160
it's maybe somewhere here, then
let's just use the end bits.

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:34.170
Let's be wasteful.

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:36.480
Maybe we can get away
with less, but let's say.

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:41.460
Now so it's just some
binary sequence of length n.

00:30:41.460 --> 00:30:43.320
Definitely, I don't
need more than,

00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:48.780
so I'm just going to use
n bits to describe it

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:59.450
if it's not typical

00:30:59.450 --> 00:31:12.860
So for this scheme, what
is the expected length,

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:19.130
the expected number of
bits that this scheme

00:31:19.130 --> 00:31:26.900
will require for encoding, for
describing the source n-tuple

00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:28.520
xn?

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:39.140
So it's essentially, well,
it's going to be one bit.

00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:47.200
It's going to be definitely
no more than 1 bit.

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:49.570
Does the one bit indicate
whether a sequence is typical

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:50.070
or not?

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:57.160
And then plus with
all likelihood,

00:31:57.160 --> 00:32:01.650
with probability
essentially 1, then I'm

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:06.960
going to need n times h bits.

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:12.360
And then I also need to
account into this expectation

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:16.830
the really small probability,
let's call it epsilon n.

00:32:19.550 --> 00:32:29.210
This is the probability that
the sequence is not typical.

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:37.745
On that event, I'm
going to need n bits.

00:32:44.990 --> 00:32:51.020
And in terms of then on
average expected number of bits

00:32:51.020 --> 00:32:55.310
per source symbol,
so I'm normalizing

00:32:55.310 --> 00:32:59.180
by the number of source
symbols that I'm describing.

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:13.200
So this is going to be about h.

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:19.350
So for n, as n grows,
this is going to be,

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:23.670
again, this is going to be
extremely small, because this

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:29.460
is the probability of being
atypical, and this is vanishing.

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:30.900
So with this kind
of theme, we are

00:33:30.900 --> 00:33:33.120
seeing that we can
get arbitrarily

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:38.475
close to achieving the entropy.

00:33:41.650 --> 00:34:01.420
As we work with larger
and larger blocks of data,

00:34:01.420 --> 00:34:02.455
everybody OK with this?

00:34:15.639 --> 00:34:25.030
So this AEP-- by the way, why
is it called the Asymptotic

00:34:25.030 --> 00:34:26.260
Equipartition Property?

00:34:26.260 --> 00:34:32.139
Because of this property
that asymptotic because it's

00:34:32.139 --> 00:34:35.500
sort of when n is
large enough, so kind

00:34:35.500 --> 00:34:38.770
of the law of large
numbers starts kicking in,

00:34:38.770 --> 00:34:44.139
then essentially what we have
is that all the action happens

00:34:44.139 --> 00:34:51.580
in this small subset
where effectively we

00:34:51.580 --> 00:34:56.170
have a uniform distribution
on the typical sequences.

00:34:56.170 --> 00:34:58.750
They each have probability
about 2 to the minus nh.

00:35:02.160 --> 00:35:05.640
And also, in fact,
implicit in this

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:11.790
AEP is the fact that we
can come close, arbitrarily

00:35:11.790 --> 00:35:17.760
close to compressing with
no more than essentially h

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:25.380
the source entropy bits per
source sequence, per component,

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:29.160
but it also actually implies--

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:37.690
or let me ask you,
could we do better?

00:35:37.690 --> 00:35:42.660
Like potentially can we do
better than h the entropy?

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:45.000
Can we get away with
lossless compression

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:49.830
with less than h bits
per source symbol?

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:59.470
And actually implicit in
everything that we just did is

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:02.890
the fact that you cannot.

00:36:02.890 --> 00:36:04.240
So let's think about this.

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:07.090
So can we do better?

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:17.610
So consider any compressor.

00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:25.930
So you give me a compressor,
and let's say that it

00:36:25.930 --> 00:36:35.740
uses n times r bits on average.

00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:39.250
Let's say it uses nr bits to
describe the source sequence.

00:36:42.380 --> 00:36:45.890
So in other words r
bits per source symbol.

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:02.780
Let me just say this, that
basically any compressor

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:07.640
that you give me
that uses nr bits,

00:37:07.640 --> 00:37:22.800
it's going to be
able to represent 2

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:27.510
to the nr possible sequences.

00:37:37.710 --> 00:37:44.550
So now what if r is less than h?

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:56.100
So what if your compressor is
using r bits per source symbol

00:37:56.100 --> 00:37:58.770
when r is less than h?

00:38:04.130 --> 00:38:08.396
Then let's say that this is--

00:38:11.900 --> 00:38:15.710
so if you're only using
nr bits, there's only--

00:38:15.710 --> 00:38:16.414
I don't know.

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:31.710
Denote this as basically this
is a set of size 2 to the nr

00:38:31.710 --> 00:38:40.290
corresponding to the different
possible sequences that this--

00:38:40.290 --> 00:38:42.000
I don't whatever
this scheme was.

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:46.080
Whatever this scheme was,
it's using only nr bits,

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:50.670
and the decoder is
going to see nr bits

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:56.070
and so what the decoder is going
to decode can only be one of 2

00:38:56.070 --> 00:39:00.900
to the nr possibilities--
possible sequences.

00:39:00.900 --> 00:39:08.580
So there's like this set
of possible sequences

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:16.600
that the decoder can output,
and if r is less than h

00:39:16.600 --> 00:39:20.530
then, in fact, this
set is necessarily

00:39:20.530 --> 00:39:23.920
a very negligible probability.

00:39:23.920 --> 00:39:24.490
Why?

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:32.410
Because this part of
the set is atypical.

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:35.920
I mean, this part has
about probability one,

00:39:35.920 --> 00:39:37.930
I mean, this whole
part is negligible.

00:39:37.930 --> 00:39:39.760
Of course, this
part is negligible.

00:39:39.760 --> 00:39:45.790
And then this part its size
is even less than 2 to the nr,

00:39:45.790 --> 00:39:50.350
to the nr if r is less than
h is of negligible size

00:39:50.350 --> 00:39:55.330
relative to this typical set
where we have essentially

00:39:55.330 --> 00:39:56.350
a uniform distribution.

00:39:56.350 --> 00:39:59.080
So I've got essentially a
uniform distribution here,

00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:07.850
and this is a very small
in size part of it.

00:40:07.850 --> 00:40:11.150
So this thing also has a
really small probability.

00:40:11.150 --> 00:40:15.480
So for now, we're not going to
get more rigorous than that,

00:40:15.480 --> 00:40:20.450
but I hope that at
least qualitatively,

00:40:20.450 --> 00:40:25.820
intuitively you're
with me, that this set

00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:27.620
of size 2 to the nr,
whatever it might

00:40:27.620 --> 00:40:29.630
be whatever your
compressor might be trying

00:40:29.630 --> 00:40:38.640
to do necessarily, the
probability that it will succeed

00:40:38.640 --> 00:40:42.910
is the probability that
the source sequence falls

00:40:42.910 --> 00:40:47.520
in some set whose
size is 2 to the nr.

00:40:47.520 --> 00:40:49.470
If r is less than
h, it's necessarily

00:40:49.470 --> 00:40:53.970
a really, really negligible set
in terms of its probability.

00:41:08.320 --> 00:41:12.010
So let me just leave
it at this compressor.

00:41:15.160 --> 00:41:19.300
Cannot be lossless.

00:41:19.300 --> 00:41:24.370
No way in hell that this
is a lossless compressor.

00:41:24.370 --> 00:41:30.580
Not only is it not lossless
with high probability,

00:41:30.580 --> 00:41:36.910
not only is it with
high probability,

00:41:36.910 --> 00:41:41.150
it's going to screw up
because with high probability,

00:41:41.150 --> 00:41:43.370
the realized
sequence is not going

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:47.780
to be in the set of sequences
that this compressor knows

00:41:47.780 --> 00:41:48.920
how to reconstruct.

00:42:11.990 --> 00:42:15.710
Is everybody OK with
this at least on a--

00:42:15.710 --> 00:42:18.640
at least on a qualitative level?

00:42:18.640 --> 00:42:27.110
Why this AEP tells us that we
can achieve compression lossless

00:42:27.110 --> 00:42:31.430
compression on average
arbitrarily close to--

00:42:31.430 --> 00:42:36.530
with rate in terms of
bits per source component.

00:42:36.530 --> 00:42:41.000
Arbitrarily close to
h, the source entropy.

00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:47.930
And that no scheme in the world
that tries to operate at a rate

00:42:47.930 --> 00:42:50.870
less than the entropy
can be lossless.

00:42:55.790 --> 00:42:57.230
Yes.

00:42:57.230 --> 00:42:59.930
Just a quick question,
so you mentioned

00:42:59.930 --> 00:43:06.290
how this AEP can get
arbitrarily close to entropy.

00:43:06.290 --> 00:43:08.990
Is this better performance
than when we were just--

00:43:08.990 --> 00:43:11.810
because when we do the
block codes of size n,

00:43:11.810 --> 00:43:14.000
we could still get the
entropy within 1 over n,

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:17.840
and here we'll also add
the entry within 1 over n.

00:43:17.840 --> 00:43:21.170
Is this better than that
or are they the same?

00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:24.800
That's a great question
and in fact Shubham

00:43:24.800 --> 00:43:29.660
is going to very shortly
address it with some plots

00:43:29.660 --> 00:43:36.020
where we've actually realized
both this kind of compressor,

00:43:36.020 --> 00:43:39.440
which we're calling
a typical encoder

00:43:39.440 --> 00:43:41.270
and decoder or a
typical compressor,

00:43:41.270 --> 00:43:47.150
what you're suggesting
it corresponds to,

00:43:47.150 --> 00:43:52.100
for example, using Shannon code.

00:43:58.220 --> 00:44:02.960
And that's another way of seeing
how we can come arbitrarily

00:44:02.960 --> 00:44:05.990
close to achieving the entropy.

00:44:05.990 --> 00:44:11.500
Like with the Shannon code
you're going to take then

00:44:11.500 --> 00:44:19.660
the length function associated
with your n-tuple is going to be

00:44:19.660 --> 00:44:23.330
log 1 over or let's
call it minus--

00:44:27.650 --> 00:44:39.230
log of 1 over the
probability of xn be precise.

00:44:39.230 --> 00:44:41.540
This is going to
be your code length

00:44:41.540 --> 00:44:52.670
which is no more than
within 1 bit of this,

00:44:52.670 --> 00:44:57.830
and so right what's going to be
your expected code length if you

00:44:57.830 --> 00:44:59.060
use Shannon code--

00:45:11.570 --> 00:45:15.880
what is this expectation?

00:45:19.880 --> 00:45:25.700
What's the
expectation of the log

00:45:25.700 --> 00:45:28.910
of 1 over the
probability of something?

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:33.130
Exactly.

00:45:33.130 --> 00:45:39.760
So in this case, this is the
entropy of this something.

00:45:44.010 --> 00:45:52.850
And if the components are
IID, then and I think--

00:45:52.850 --> 00:45:54.590
did we say this
explicitly or will

00:45:54.590 --> 00:45:57.980
this be an exercise
that the sum--

00:45:57.980 --> 00:46:02.675
that the entropy of an
n-tuple with IID components--

00:46:02.675 --> 00:46:04.140
[INAUDIBLE]

00:46:04.140 --> 00:46:04.640
OK.

00:46:04.640 --> 00:46:05.270
Great.

00:46:05.270 --> 00:46:06.710
Great.

00:46:06.710 --> 00:46:11.150
So this you know is
nothing but n times

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:16.310
the entropy of each of
the individual components

00:46:16.310 --> 00:46:18.140
because they have
the same distribution

00:46:18.140 --> 00:46:23.660
and they're independent,
and so absolutely.

00:46:23.660 --> 00:46:29.270
So here too you
see a scheme whose

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:46.040
number of bits per source symbol
comes arbitrarily close to--

00:46:46.040 --> 00:46:47.300
oops, sorry.

00:46:47.300 --> 00:46:49.060
1 over n times n times hx.

00:46:52.760 --> 00:46:55.370
So with the Shannon
code, you can also

00:46:55.370 --> 00:47:00.770
come arbitrarily close to
achieving the source entropy

00:47:00.770 --> 00:47:04.700
if you apply it,
construct it and apply it

00:47:04.700 --> 00:47:08.780
on a sufficiently
large block of data.

00:47:08.780 --> 00:47:12.080
And which of them is better?

00:47:12.080 --> 00:47:15.140
You're going to see
some plots very shortly,

00:47:15.140 --> 00:47:19.430
and you're also going to see
that in practice, this is not

00:47:19.430 --> 00:47:23.180
what we want to be doing
to get close to the entropy

00:47:23.180 --> 00:47:27.800
because the complexity of
these schemes both in time

00:47:27.800 --> 00:47:30.920
and in memory is
growing exponentially.

00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:37.910
We need lookup tables that
are growing exponentially,

00:47:37.910 --> 00:47:40.730
even though it's only let's
say the size of a typical set

00:47:40.730 --> 00:47:44.240
with a typical encoder,
still and it's not

00:47:44.240 --> 00:47:46.010
the set of all
sequences but still it's

00:47:46.010 --> 00:47:48.770
an exponentially
growing set with n.

00:47:48.770 --> 00:47:56.720
So it's not very
realistic when n is large,

00:47:56.720 --> 00:47:59.810
and we're going to talk about
how to actually get arbitrarily

00:47:59.810 --> 00:48:04.550
close to this thing with
very reasonable complexity.

00:48:04.550 --> 00:48:07.220
Very shortly, the
last thing that I

00:48:07.220 --> 00:48:12.920
will do speaking
of the Shannon code

00:48:12.920 --> 00:48:24.050
is briefly talk about
mismatched compression.

00:48:24.050 --> 00:48:32.320
So let's say you knew
that your compressor--

00:48:34.910 --> 00:48:45.875
so let's consider a compressor
with a length function.

00:48:49.660 --> 00:48:52.760
Let's say generically now,
it's just like one element.

00:48:52.760 --> 00:48:53.980
Forget n-tuples.

00:48:53.980 --> 00:48:56.680
Now you've got an
element in a set

00:48:56.680 --> 00:49:00.790
that you want to
devise a compressor,

00:49:00.790 --> 00:49:04.330
a lossless compressor
for, and so

00:49:04.330 --> 00:49:11.290
we know that you can always
devise a Shannon code assuming

00:49:11.290 --> 00:49:12.580
that it has some distribution.

00:49:12.580 --> 00:49:16.210
Let's say you assume it has a
distribution of probability mass

00:49:16.210 --> 00:49:24.960
function q, then we can always
construct a Shannon code that

00:49:24.960 --> 00:49:32.720
will have this length function.

00:49:32.720 --> 00:49:38.620
And very precisely with
an upper integer value.

00:49:38.620 --> 00:49:42.840
But essentially, what's
one bit among friends,

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:50.000
let's just say roughly
up to that one bit.

00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:54.080
If I thought that
what I'm compressing

00:49:54.080 --> 00:49:58.280
is governed by a distribution
q probability mass function q,

00:49:58.280 --> 00:50:01.120
then I would construct--

00:50:01.120 --> 00:50:04.580
I would try to construct a
compressor whose length function

00:50:04.580 --> 00:50:10.090
essentially log 1 over q
because we saw that if it were

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:12.640
distributed q,
then this is going

00:50:12.640 --> 00:50:18.550
to get me close to the
entropy of that thing.

00:50:18.550 --> 00:50:23.000
So but what if--

00:50:23.000 --> 00:50:26.180
so I constructed
a compressor that

00:50:26.180 --> 00:50:30.890
assumes that it's coming from
a probability mass function q

00:50:30.890 --> 00:50:42.050
when, in fact, x,
in fact, God decided

00:50:42.050 --> 00:50:46.760
that she wants to
generate it according

00:50:46.760 --> 00:50:48.575
to some other distribution p.

00:50:55.160 --> 00:51:04.170
Then what's the price
I'm going to pay?

00:51:04.170 --> 00:51:08.440
What's a price that I'm paying
for constructing a compressor

00:51:08.440 --> 00:51:10.410
assuming it came
from a distribution q

00:51:10.410 --> 00:51:13.110
while it was actually
distributed p?

00:51:13.110 --> 00:51:15.450
Well, let's see what my--

00:51:18.660 --> 00:51:20.910
what the expected number
of bits that I'm going

00:51:20.910 --> 00:51:23.190
to need under this scheme.

00:51:25.890 --> 00:51:31.740
Well, it's the expectation,
so all the possible values

00:51:31.740 --> 00:51:34.530
for x, it's distributed p.

00:51:34.530 --> 00:51:46.240
So the expectation is the
probability of x times l of x.

00:51:46.240 --> 00:51:55.500
That's probability of x log 2.

00:51:55.500 --> 00:52:02.665
So this is log 1 over q.

00:52:06.730 --> 00:52:12.745
You will allow me to say inside
the log multiply it by 1.

00:52:24.550 --> 00:52:29.740
So what am I going to get,
some over the possible values

00:52:29.740 --> 00:52:49.130
of x, px log 1 over px
plus the sum over xpx log--

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:50.130
what do I still owe you?

00:52:50.130 --> 00:52:54.810
So I've got log 1 over p is
here, so now what remains

00:52:54.810 --> 00:53:01.415
is p over q log of p over q.

00:53:08.350 --> 00:53:13.900
So what's this guy?

00:53:13.900 --> 00:53:14.770
Look familiar?

00:53:20.460 --> 00:53:25.380
Exactly this is
the entropy of x.

00:53:25.380 --> 00:53:36.430
And this guy sum over the
possible values of xpx log px

00:53:36.430 --> 00:53:41.530
over qx is what's known--

00:53:41.530 --> 00:53:45.160
in fact, we have
a notation for it.

00:53:45.160 --> 00:53:46.105
We denote it.

00:53:49.990 --> 00:53:53.020
It's denoted.

00:53:53.020 --> 00:53:58.870
We also defined it as part of
the proof or proof [INAUDIBLE]

00:53:58.870 --> 00:54:00.560
the lower bound
proof [INAUDIBLE]..

00:54:00.560 --> 00:54:01.060
OK.

00:54:01.060 --> 00:54:02.470
Great.

00:54:02.470 --> 00:54:04.180
So what is this, guys?

00:54:04.180 --> 00:54:05.035
What is this?

00:54:05.035 --> 00:54:07.060
It's entropy.

00:54:07.060 --> 00:54:11.110
This is the relative entropy
or Kullback-Leibler divergence

00:54:11.110 --> 00:54:13.120
between p and q.

00:54:13.120 --> 00:54:21.190
So we're seeing that
obviously we already know

00:54:21.190 --> 00:54:24.520
what you can't do
better than the entropy,

00:54:24.520 --> 00:54:29.320
but what we're seeing here
is that this relative entropy

00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:33.610
between p and q is
the price of mismatch.

00:54:33.610 --> 00:54:37.840
It's the price in terms
of excess number of bits

00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:44.950
that you're going to have to
pay for compressing something

00:54:44.950 --> 00:54:47.770
that came from distribution
p when you designed

00:54:47.770 --> 00:54:52.010
a compressor that assumes it
came from a distribution q.

00:54:52.010 --> 00:54:56.480
And so obviously, you can never
do better than the entropy,

00:54:56.480 --> 00:54:57.530
and if you assumed--

00:54:57.530 --> 00:55:00.595
and if you did something--

00:55:00.595 --> 00:55:01.970
assumed a different
distribution,

00:55:01.970 --> 00:55:04.340
you're obviously going
to pay, so this thing

00:55:04.340 --> 00:55:11.750
is always non-negative, and, in
fact, it's going to be positive

00:55:11.750 --> 00:55:18.290
and growing the more
different q is from p.

00:55:20.810 --> 00:55:23.870
I think I'm going
to leave it at that

00:55:23.870 --> 00:55:29.015
and let Shubham now show you
some really interesting plots.

