00:58:08.670 --> 00:58:11.700
for this block size.

00:58:11.700 --> 00:58:13.830
So for the Bernoulli
0.01 source,

00:58:13.830 --> 00:58:16.650
we show the red dotted
line at the bottom that

00:58:16.650 --> 00:58:18.990
shows the entropy,
so that's basically

00:58:18.990 --> 00:58:20.400
the best you can ever do.

00:58:20.400 --> 00:58:26.340
And the purple dotted line,
that is the x plus 1 by n bound

00:58:26.340 --> 00:58:29.250
that you just saw here.

00:58:29.250 --> 00:58:30.870
So that bound is for
Shannon code also

00:58:30.870 --> 00:58:32.940
applies to Huffman codes.

00:58:32.940 --> 00:58:36.510
So you see here that all
three codes do pretty well.

00:58:36.510 --> 00:58:39.150
They are very close
to each other.

00:58:39.150 --> 00:58:41.700
Huffman codes are the best
because we know that they

00:58:41.700 --> 00:58:44.970
are the optimal codes.

00:58:44.970 --> 00:58:46.530
So they are at the bottom.

00:58:46.530 --> 00:58:49.440
And then typical and
Shannon, there is sort of a--

00:58:49.440 --> 00:58:51.370
depending on the block
size, one or the other

00:58:51.370 --> 00:58:53.590
does better, so this is good.

00:58:53.590 --> 00:58:55.450
The things we saw
there it's gradually

00:58:55.450 --> 00:58:56.740
getting close to entropy.

00:58:56.740 --> 00:59:00.250
It's not quite there
at a block size of 10,

00:59:00.250 --> 00:59:03.300
but it will get there because
we have that boundary that's

00:59:03.300 --> 00:59:06.520
x plus 1 by n, so we know
that at block size 20,

00:59:06.520 --> 00:59:09.010
you will get within
0.05 of entropy,

00:59:09.010 --> 00:59:11.425
and eventually, you will
get very, very close.

00:59:13.990 --> 00:59:21.080
So all three codes have
exponential complexity just

00:59:21.080 --> 00:59:24.830
for constructing the codebook
because the typical set

00:59:24.830 --> 00:59:27.560
code you enumerate over all the
sequences find the typical ones

00:59:27.560 --> 00:59:29.900
that's exponential complexity.

00:59:29.900 --> 00:59:33.290
Shannon code, again, you have
to go through every sequence.

00:59:33.290 --> 00:59:36.260
Just building that tree is
exponential complexity for all

00:59:36.260 --> 00:59:37.190
three of them.

00:59:37.190 --> 00:59:40.250
And then storing that tree
or storing the codebook

00:59:40.250 --> 00:59:41.060
in your memory.

00:59:41.060 --> 00:59:46.880
That's also exponential,
so we also plot below.

00:59:46.880 --> 00:59:49.490
This shows the time
for code generation,

00:59:49.490 --> 00:59:51.650
so generating the
code book or the tree

00:59:51.650 --> 00:59:55.980
versus block size
for the same source.

00:59:55.980 --> 00:59:59.790
Hoffman is really like
dominating all of this.

00:59:59.790 --> 01:00:02.490
I wouldn't worry too much
about their relative scaling

01:00:02.490 --> 01:00:04.663
because that also depends
on our implementation.

01:00:04.663 --> 01:00:06.330
Huffman code is just
doing a lot of work

01:00:06.330 --> 01:00:10.420
with that particular algorithm
to merge things and so on.

01:00:10.420 --> 01:00:14.250
So to maybe make
it easier to see,

01:00:14.250 --> 01:00:16.830
here I put the time
on a log scale.

01:00:16.830 --> 01:00:19.320
So a linear growth
on a log scale

01:00:19.320 --> 01:00:22.890
is exponential growth
in the normal thing.

01:00:22.890 --> 01:00:25.170
So you see all three
are going basically

01:00:25.170 --> 01:00:26.670
linearly on this scale.

01:00:26.670 --> 01:00:28.980
So all three are exponential.

01:00:28.980 --> 01:00:32.490
None of them is going
to scale very well.

01:00:32.490 --> 01:00:34.738
And if you want to use
these codes, basically, what

01:00:34.738 --> 01:00:36.780
will happen is you will
need to restrict yourself

01:00:36.780 --> 01:00:40.560
to some smallish block length,
and you will deprive yourself

01:00:40.560 --> 01:00:42.570
of actually achieving entropy.

01:00:42.570 --> 01:00:47.190
So that's just not
ideal situation.

01:00:47.190 --> 01:00:49.680
So what you will learn
in the next two lectures

01:00:49.680 --> 01:00:53.250
is a way to achieve entropy.

01:00:53.250 --> 01:00:55.290
Effectively, have
very big block sizes

01:00:55.290 --> 01:00:58.150
without having this
exponential complexity.

01:00:58.150 --> 01:01:01.120
So that will be the
