01:02:47.270 --> 01:02:51.590
So if you have already
looked at question 5

01:02:51.590 --> 01:02:54.260
in your homework, that talks
about if you have a Huffman

01:02:54.260 --> 01:02:57.830
code, you need to when you
send it to the decoder,

01:02:57.830 --> 01:02:59.870
how will the decoder
know what Huffman code

01:02:59.870 --> 01:03:01.130
is, the encoder using?

01:03:01.130 --> 01:03:03.770
So there should be a way to
either you send the probability

01:03:03.770 --> 01:03:07.370
distribution over or you
send the Huffman code over,

01:03:07.370 --> 01:03:09.680
but there is a
third possibility.

01:03:09.680 --> 01:03:17.240
The third possibility is
shown in section 3.2.6

01:03:17.240 --> 01:03:20.720
which is compression
with fixed Huffman codes.

01:03:20.720 --> 01:03:22.910
So in many cases,
the gzip files,

01:03:22.910 --> 01:03:25.610
they just have a hard-coded
Huffman code in them.

01:03:25.610 --> 01:03:27.110
They just looked
at a lot of data,

01:03:27.110 --> 01:03:30.260
found a reasonable
distribution, and it just

01:03:30.260 --> 01:03:31.820
uses that standard Huffman code.

01:03:31.820 --> 01:03:34.370
Both the encoder and the
decoder have that in there--

01:03:34.370 --> 01:03:37.980
basically, it's part
of the format itself.

01:03:37.980 --> 01:03:39.920
So in a lot of
practical applications,

01:03:39.920 --> 01:03:43.190
you will see that you don't
try to learn the Huffman

01:03:43.190 --> 01:03:46.490
tree on the fly, you just
use a standard Huffman tree,

01:03:46.490 --> 01:03:51.740
which you learn basically
designed beforehand.

01:03:51.740 --> 01:03:53.720
It's like a hyperparameter
sort of thing,

01:03:53.720 --> 01:03:56.480
where you just
fix it beforehand.

01:03:56.480 --> 01:03:59.900
Then you could also have
a dynamic Huffman code.

01:03:59.900 --> 01:04:02.600
So both are supported
in the format.

01:04:02.600 --> 01:04:04.880
So now the question
comes like, how

01:04:04.880 --> 01:04:07.160
do you represent the
dynamic Huffman code?

01:04:07.160 --> 01:04:10.040
How do you transmit it
to the decoder side?

01:04:10.040 --> 01:04:12.260
So in the homework problem,
what you are doing is

01:04:12.260 --> 01:04:15.260
you are encoding the probability
distribution sending it over,

01:04:15.260 --> 01:04:18.170
and then the decoder decodes
the probability distribution

01:04:18.170 --> 01:04:23.450
and builds the Huffman
tree on the decoder side.

01:04:23.450 --> 01:04:26.270
Gzip does not do that,
and the main reason

01:04:26.270 --> 01:04:27.920
is like if you
look at the gzip, I

01:04:27.920 --> 01:04:31.010
encourage you, maybe not
now, maybe after lecture 10,

01:04:31.010 --> 01:04:34.340
read this entire RFC which
describes the gzip format,

01:04:34.340 --> 01:04:37.130
and you will see how
for each and every bit

01:04:37.130 --> 01:04:39.650
they are like think like twice.

01:04:39.650 --> 01:04:44.630
How can I avoid spending this
one bit while being super fast?

01:04:44.630 --> 01:04:46.860
So it's very interesting.

