This is basically
equivalent Huffman code

01:05:47.820 --> 01:05:51.160
to the one on the
left, and it will

01:05:51.160 --> 01:05:53.410
get you the same expected
code length and everything.

01:05:53.410 --> 01:05:55.750
So Huffman codes are not unique.

01:05:55.750 --> 01:05:58.150
When you're breaking ties,
when you're building the tree,

01:05:58.150 --> 01:05:59.698
you have some
flexibility, and then

01:05:59.698 --> 01:06:01.240
when you're putting,
which one to put

01:06:01.240 --> 01:06:02.410
on left, which one
to put on right,

01:06:02.410 --> 01:06:03.640
there is some flexibility.

01:06:03.640 --> 01:06:06.730
So just generally, you will
see that the algorithm is not

01:06:06.730 --> 01:06:08.230
like fully deterministic.

01:06:08.230 --> 01:06:10.150
There are multiple Huffman
codes corresponding

01:06:10.150 --> 01:06:12.640
to the same distribution.

01:06:12.640 --> 01:06:15.450
So that's what makes
transmitting it so hard.

01:06:15.450 --> 01:06:17.200
I don't know if you
started on question 5.

01:06:17.200 --> 01:06:18.730
Last year lots of
people have had

01:06:18.730 --> 01:06:22.120
issues because they
were just like,

01:06:22.120 --> 01:06:24.882
you do just need to be careful
that you do the exact same thing

01:06:24.882 --> 01:06:26.590
on the encoder and
the decoder otherwise,

01:06:26.590 --> 01:06:28.900
the whole thing breaks down.

01:06:28.900 --> 01:06:31.870
So there are a
couple of properties

01:06:31.870 --> 01:06:34.930
that you have in
the left code, which

01:06:34.930 --> 01:06:39.130
is one is that there are
longer codewords to the left

01:06:39.130 --> 01:06:43.280
and a shorter
codeword to the right.

01:06:43.280 --> 01:06:44.060
Sure.

01:06:44.060 --> 01:06:46.670
And then there is another
property that you have C and D,

01:06:46.670 --> 01:06:50.000
and C comes like
lexicographically before D. It's

01:06:50.000 --> 01:06:54.050
alphabetically smaller,
but it's to the right of D.

01:06:54.050 --> 01:06:58.550
So this code according to the
definition of canonical codes

01:06:58.550 --> 01:06:59.840
is not canonical.

01:06:59.840 --> 01:07:01.580
Canonical codes have
a few properties

01:07:01.580 --> 01:07:03.620
they are sorted by
codeword length.

01:07:03.620 --> 01:07:06.350
So anything on the left should
be shorter than anything

01:07:06.350 --> 01:07:06.920
on the right.

01:07:06.920 --> 01:07:11.150
So as you go from left to
right, your thing should grow.

01:07:11.150 --> 01:07:14.690
And if you have two
codewords of the same length,

01:07:14.690 --> 01:07:18.050
the one on the left should be
the lexicographically smaller.

01:07:18.050 --> 01:07:20.690
So C should come before
D. A should come before B

01:07:20.690 --> 01:07:22.010
if and B have the same length.

01:07:25.090 --> 01:07:28.660
So what you can show,
you can reason about it.

01:07:28.660 --> 01:07:31.120
Basically you can just
like shift around the tree,

01:07:31.120 --> 01:07:33.490
and you can show that
for any Huffman code,

01:07:33.490 --> 01:07:35.500
you can convert it into
a canonical code, which

01:07:35.500 --> 01:07:37.318
has these two
properties basically

01:07:37.318 --> 01:07:39.610
that it's sorted by codeword
length from left to right,

01:07:39.610 --> 01:07:43.900
and for the same length, it's
sorted lexicographically.

01:07:43.900 --> 01:07:45.250
Any questions on this?

01:07:51.880 --> 01:07:56.950
Let's do one
example where what I

01:07:56.950 --> 01:07:59.742
will try to show is for
a canonical Huffman code,

01:07:59.742 --> 01:08:01.450
you don't need to send
the probabilities,

01:08:01.450 --> 01:08:03.867
you don't need to send the
structure of the tree, nothing.

01:08:03.867 --> 01:08:07.300
You just need to send the
length of the codewords,

01:08:07.300 --> 01:08:09.190
and that is enough
to reconstruct

01:08:09.190 --> 01:08:11.230
the codebook basically.

01:08:11.230 --> 01:08:13.690
So let's try to do this.

01:08:13.690 --> 01:08:16.913
So you start with
the smallest one.

01:08:16.913 --> 01:08:18.330
So this is the one
you start with.

01:08:18.330 --> 01:08:19.770
B equal to 1.

01:08:19.770 --> 01:08:22.290
And you know that this
has to be to the left

01:08:22.290 --> 01:08:26.640
of every other codeword
so first thing you do

01:08:26.640 --> 01:08:31.670
is that this B has to be
on left of everything.

01:08:31.670 --> 01:08:34.830
Which is the next
one I should pick?

01:08:34.830 --> 01:08:36.060
A.

01:08:36.060 --> 01:08:41.300
A. Can anybody tell what
is the code word for A?

01:08:41.300 --> 01:08:44.149
Yeah, it has to be 1 0 because
we know that we are always

01:08:44.149 --> 01:08:48.649
going left to right, so this is
the only possibility basically.

01:08:48.649 --> 01:08:51.020
So A.

01:08:51.020 --> 01:08:57.043
Now I have both C and
D which are length 3.

01:08:57.043 --> 01:08:58.460
So which one should
go on the left

01:08:58.460 --> 01:09:00.155
and which one should
go on the right?

01:09:03.262 --> 01:09:05.720
Yeah, C has to be on the left
again because of the property

01:09:05.720 --> 01:09:07.910
of canonical codes.

01:09:07.910 --> 01:09:10.340
So if you have a
canonical code, you

01:09:10.340 --> 01:09:11.990
just need the lengths
of the code words

01:09:11.990 --> 01:09:16.340
to be able to reconstruct
the entire code.

01:09:16.340 --> 01:09:18.410
So it's a very, very
efficient representation

01:09:18.410 --> 01:09:20.420
as opposed to storing
probabilities.

01:09:20.420 --> 01:09:23.990
Also cheap to reconstruct
on the decoder side.

01:09:23.990 --> 01:09:28.930
It's a very simple algorithm
to get back to the codebook.

01:09:28.930 --> 01:09:30.115
Any questions on this?

01:09:36.850 --> 01:09:40.060
So gzip uses canonical
Huffman codes

01:09:40.060 --> 01:09:44.640
when they're using this
dynamic coding strategy.

01:09:44.640 --> 01:09:48.952
Can anybody suggest how you
would store these lengths?

01:09:48.952 --> 01:09:50.910
Any ideas on how you
would actually because you

01:09:50.910 --> 01:09:52.175
need to transmit the lengths.

01:09:52.175 --> 01:09:54.300
You need to put the lengths
in the compressed file.

01:09:54.300 --> 01:09:55.530
How would you store the lengths?

01:09:55.530 --> 01:09:56.100
Any ideas?

01:10:03.280 --> 01:10:06.370
For example, let's say I know
that the lengths are at most 16

01:10:06.370 --> 01:10:09.670
or let's the lengths are at
most 8, so how would you--

01:10:09.670 --> 01:10:11.170
how could you
represent the lengths?

01:10:19.830 --> 01:10:20.610
It's not a trick.

01:10:20.610 --> 01:10:23.430
It's a simple question
from what you've learned.

01:10:27.680 --> 01:10:29.870
So the lengths have to
be a prefix-free code

01:10:29.870 --> 01:10:33.920
so that the other side
can reconstruct it right.

01:10:33.920 --> 01:10:37.750
So one simple thing you could
do is you just store with--

01:10:37.750 --> 01:10:39.550
let's say if the
maximum length is 8,

01:10:39.550 --> 01:10:41.920
you just do like
3 bits per length,

01:10:41.920 --> 01:10:44.080
and you have however
many lengths,

01:10:44.080 --> 01:10:45.565
you just transmit that way.

01:10:49.040 --> 01:10:54.170
Obviously, as I said, gzip
is very, very concerned

01:10:54.170 --> 01:10:57.230
about being very efficient.

01:11:06.940 --> 01:11:10.132
So what gzip does
is the Huffman codes

01:11:10.132 --> 01:11:11.840
for the two alphabets
appear in the block

01:11:11.840 --> 01:11:13.940
immediately after
the header, whatever.

01:11:13.940 --> 01:11:16.580
Each code is defined by a
sequence of code lengths

01:11:16.580 --> 01:11:18.440
using the canonical description.

01:11:18.440 --> 01:11:21.620
For even greater
compactness, the code length

01:11:21.620 --> 01:11:25.340
sequences themselves are
compressed using a Huffman code.

01:11:25.340 --> 01:11:27.350
So you compress your
data with Huffman code,

01:11:27.350 --> 01:11:29.870
and then to describe
the Huffman code,

01:11:29.870 --> 01:11:32.770
you store that with
another Huffman code.

01:11:32.770 --> 01:11:37.640
So you will see how much they're
like concerned about the space.

01:11:37.640 --> 01:11:42.110
And the way gzip does it
is it uses a static Huffman

01:11:42.110 --> 01:11:43.400
code for storing the lengths.

01:11:43.400 --> 01:11:46.100
So in gzip what happens is--

01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:52.280
so you have the whatever stream.

01:11:52.280 --> 01:12:00.240
So that's stored with
a dynamic Huffman code,

01:12:00.240 --> 01:12:05.000
and then you need to store the
lengths of this thing, which

01:12:05.000 --> 01:12:07.610
are stored with a
static Huffman code.

01:12:07.610 --> 01:12:12.900
So it's a Huffman code whose
table is fixed basically.

01:12:12.900 --> 01:12:16.140
So just like--
there are different

01:12:16.140 --> 01:12:18.600
ways people use these
things, but in gzip, this

01:12:18.600 --> 01:12:19.800
is how it's done.

01:12:23.890 --> 01:12:25.090
Any questions?

01:12:36.230 --> 01:12:39.530
In the remaining time, let's
go over the quiz quickly.

01:12:42.980 --> 01:12:46.780
I hope most of these, you will
be able to help me out with.

01:12:46.780 --> 01:12:49.990
So we had a question 1.

01:12:49.990 --> 01:12:53.920
We asked you to construct the
Huffman code for Bernoulli 0.5.

01:12:53.920 --> 01:12:54.710
Anybody?

01:13:00.210 --> 01:13:01.310
0 1.

01:13:01.310 --> 01:13:03.690
Yeah, just like
it's a simple code.

01:13:03.690 --> 01:13:09.210
Not much you can do
for just two symbols.

01:13:09.210 --> 01:13:10.190
Bernoulli 0.9?

01:13:12.860 --> 01:13:14.390
Yeah, it's the same thing.

01:13:14.390 --> 01:13:15.030
OK.

01:13:15.030 --> 01:13:15.530
Good.

01:13:20.350 --> 01:13:20.850
OK.

01:13:20.850 --> 01:13:22.050
1.3.

01:13:22.050 --> 01:13:23.475
How many think it's true?

01:13:28.450 --> 01:13:30.100
A lot of people think it's true.

01:13:30.100 --> 01:13:31.630
And it is true.

01:13:31.630 --> 01:13:34.810
Why is it true?

01:13:34.810 --> 01:13:39.010
Because we're [INAUDIBLE]
because it's like 1.5.

01:13:39.010 --> 01:13:42.310
Yeah, so true because
entropy is 1 in this case,

01:13:42.310 --> 01:13:45.400
and so you can't do better.

01:13:45.400 --> 01:13:46.490
How about 1.4?

01:13:49.660 --> 01:13:51.340
I see some heads shaking.

01:13:51.340 --> 01:13:58.180
So the answer is false because
entropy is less than 1,

01:13:58.180 --> 01:13:59.998
how do you square
this with the fact

01:13:59.998 --> 01:14:01.915
that Huffman codes are
supposed to be optimal?

01:14:05.250 --> 01:14:08.010
We can do a block codes.

01:14:08.010 --> 01:14:13.770
Yeah, so I'm hearing that we can
do block codes which is true.

01:14:13.770 --> 01:14:16.050
If you are forced to do
just one symbol at a time,

01:14:16.050 --> 01:14:18.022
then this is indeed
optimal, but we

01:14:18.022 --> 01:14:19.980
didn't say that you are
forced to do that here.

01:14:19.980 --> 01:14:24.270
So if you assume that and
then you wrote the answer,

01:14:24.270 --> 01:14:26.310
I guess talk to us, we'll see.

01:14:26.310 --> 01:14:30.960
But yeah, so basically you
can do block codes here.

01:14:30.960 --> 01:14:33.840
And we saw in that
example how it was

01:14:33.840 --> 01:14:35.280
dropping with the block size.

01:14:39.930 --> 01:14:43.330
Any questions on this?

01:14:43.330 --> 01:14:45.740
Let's move on.

01:14:45.740 --> 01:14:47.630
Then we have the
second question where

01:14:47.630 --> 01:14:51.800
you had an alphabet with four
symbols and uniform distribution

01:14:51.800 --> 01:14:52.910
over the four.

01:14:52.910 --> 01:14:58.160
So the Huffman code as you know
for a uniform distribution,

01:14:58.160 --> 01:15:02.570
it will be a fixed-length code,
for a uniform distribution

01:15:02.570 --> 01:15:04.580
over like four symbols.

01:15:04.580 --> 01:15:07.810
So we have the
Huffman code here.

01:15:07.810 --> 01:15:09.310
And then there was
this other code

01:15:09.310 --> 01:15:17.290
that it's a prefix-free code,
no particular properties as yet.

01:15:17.290 --> 01:15:19.840
And then we said you have a
particular sequence AAABBBCD.

01:15:23.260 --> 01:15:27.460
So first part was like the
encoded length with Gandhi code,

01:15:27.460 --> 01:15:40.870
and you can actually do the
encoding 000101010110111.

01:15:40.870 --> 01:15:43.960
So I'm getting 15, I
hope that's correct.

01:15:43.960 --> 01:15:50.690
And then for Huffman code,
you have 8 symbols, 2, so 16.

01:15:50.690 --> 01:15:53.660
So we see that in
this particular case,

01:15:53.660 --> 01:15:56.645
Huffman code was
beaten by Gandhi code.

01:16:00.400 --> 01:16:06.460
So question 2.1, Gandhi codes
are optimal for this source.

01:16:06.460 --> 01:16:07.750
How many think it's true?

01:16:12.230 --> 01:16:13.470
How many think it's false?

01:16:17.100 --> 01:16:21.430
But we saw it, we
saw that you beat it.

01:16:31.120 --> 01:16:35.730
Yeah, so I guess the last part
gives you a hint in a way, but--

01:16:35.730 --> 01:16:47.300
oh, sorry, this
should be 2.3, 2.4.

01:16:47.300 --> 01:16:49.280
So this is important
to understand.

01:16:49.280 --> 01:16:51.350
You have a source.

01:16:51.350 --> 01:16:53.450
A source is a
probabilistic source,

01:16:53.450 --> 01:16:55.940
and when we say it's
optimal, what we mean is that

01:16:55.940 --> 01:16:59.810
the expected length cannot
be better than the entropy

01:16:59.810 --> 01:17:03.530
or Huffman codes give you gives
you the minimum expected length.

01:17:03.530 --> 01:17:07.070
We don't say that for each
and every particular sequence

01:17:07.070 --> 01:17:08.150
realization.

01:17:08.150 --> 01:17:10.198
Huffman codes are
going to be better.

01:17:10.198 --> 01:17:11.240
That's just not possible.

01:17:11.240 --> 01:17:13.640
You can't have a
code that's better

01:17:13.640 --> 01:17:17.732
than every other code on every
possible sequence because

01:17:17.732 --> 01:17:19.190
in particular, what
you could do is

01:17:19.190 --> 01:17:20.660
like if you want
to only transmit

01:17:20.660 --> 01:17:22.880
one particular sequence,
what you would do is--

01:17:25.940 --> 01:17:28.235
and if you can-- if you're
allowed to use block codes,

01:17:28.235 --> 01:17:29.860
what you could do is
for that sequence,

01:17:29.860 --> 01:17:32.470
you define the code as zero,
and for the other ones,

01:17:32.470 --> 01:17:34.120
you then define whatever.

01:17:34.120 --> 01:17:36.880
So if you know like
you have to transmit

01:17:36.880 --> 01:17:40.030
this particular sequence,
you can always cheat,

01:17:40.030 --> 01:17:43.600
but the whole game is about
transmitting something useful,

01:17:43.600 --> 01:17:46.137
and it's useful only if
there is uncertainty.

01:17:46.137 --> 01:17:48.220
So that's why we work in
the probabilistic setting

01:17:48.220 --> 01:17:49.898
where the decoder
doesn't already know

01:17:49.898 --> 01:17:51.190
what you are going to transmit.

01:17:53.980 --> 01:17:55.540
This happens a
lot in compression

01:17:55.540 --> 01:17:59.093
where there are these
compression challenges where

01:17:59.093 --> 01:18:00.760
like you're given a
file, and you really

01:18:00.760 --> 01:18:03.190
want to compress it to
be as small as possible.

01:18:03.190 --> 01:18:07.270
So what I could do is I could
create a compressor decompressor

01:18:07.270 --> 01:18:11.860
pair where the compressor
just sends nothing or is just

01:18:11.860 --> 01:18:14.260
sends like one byte,
and the decompressor

01:18:14.260 --> 01:18:17.980
has the entire file as
part of the decompressor.

01:18:17.980 --> 01:18:21.340
So the decompressor just gets
that one byte, and expands it

01:18:21.340 --> 01:18:22.600
out to the entire file.

01:18:22.600 --> 01:18:25.070
So that's like a way
of cheating in a way,

01:18:25.070 --> 01:18:26.570
so that's why like
these compression

01:18:26.570 --> 01:18:28.910
challenges also
have you can't have

01:18:28.910 --> 01:18:32.570
a decompressor that's too big.

01:18:32.570 --> 01:18:35.318
So the whole thing is like
the decoder shouldn't already

01:18:35.318 --> 01:18:36.860
know what is going
to be transmitted,

01:18:36.860 --> 01:18:39.230
otherwise the decoder,
what is the point?

01:18:39.230 --> 01:18:42.110
Why are we even
sending anything?

01:18:42.110 --> 01:18:46.670
Anyway, so for this particular
instance of the source,

01:18:46.670 --> 01:18:50.780
you can actually show that if
you look at the distribution

01:18:50.780 --> 01:18:55.940
here, A occurs three
out of eight times,

01:18:55.940 --> 01:18:59.420
B occurs three out of
eight times, C is 1 over 8,

01:18:59.420 --> 01:19:01.410
D is 1 over 8.

01:19:01.410 --> 01:19:04.110
And if you use this and you
construct the Huffman code,

01:19:04.110 --> 01:19:08.100
you will see that the
Huffman code is this one.

01:19:08.100 --> 01:19:10.890
So this is the Huffman
code for this distribution,

01:19:10.890 --> 01:19:15.240
and hence it is actually
optimal for this thing.

01:19:15.240 --> 01:19:17.610
So it's important.

01:19:17.610 --> 01:19:19.590
For particular
instances, you can

01:19:19.590 --> 01:19:22.800
do things which are different
from the overall optimal source,

01:19:22.800 --> 01:19:28.740
and when we say optimal,
we mean in expectation.

01:19:28.740 --> 01:19:32.230
So this is already done today.

01:19:32.230 --> 01:19:35.620
Theoretical interaction
between entropy block coding.

01:19:35.620 --> 01:19:37.570
And as I said,
next lecture, we'll

01:19:37.570 --> 01:19:40.660
talk about practical codes which
will be useful not only to get

01:19:40.660 --> 01:19:45.920
closer to entropy, but also very
crucial for adaptive schemes,

01:19:45.920 --> 01:19:48.310
non-ID source
compression, places

01:19:48.310 --> 01:19:49.750
where you don't
want to assume you

01:19:49.750 --> 01:19:54.670
know the distribution already,
just a lot of exciting stuff

01:19:54.670 --> 01:19:56.050
coming up.

01:19:56.050 --> 01:19:57.930
Thank you.

